**The Power of Annotations:**

JUnit uses annotations to mark methods as tests and control their execution lifecycle. This declarative approach makes tests clean and easy to understand.

**Core JUnit 5 Annotations:**

| Annotation | Description | Execution Count |
|------------|-------------|-----------------|
| **@Test** | Marks a method as a test method | Once per test |
| **@BeforeEach** | Runs before each test method | Once per test |
| **@AfterEach** | Runs after each test method | Once per test |
| **@BeforeAll** | Runs once before all tests in class | Once per class |
| **@AfterAll** | Runs once after all tests in class | Once per class |
| **@Disabled** | Disables a test or test class | Never |
| **@DisplayName** | Provides a custom name for the test | N/A |
| **@Nested** | Creates a nested test class | N/A |
| **@Tag** | Tags tests for filtering | N/A |
| **@RepeatedTest** | Repeats a test specified number of times | Multiple times |
| **@ParameterizedTest** | Marks a parameterized test | Multiple times |
| **@TestFactory** | Creates dynamic tests | As defined |

**Execution Order Example:**

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.*;

class LifecycleTest {

    @BeforeAll
    static void beforeAll() {
        System.out.println("1. @BeforeAll - runs once before all tests");
    }

    @BeforeEach
    void beforeEach() {
        System.out.println("2. @BeforeEach - runs before each test");
    }

    @Test
    void testOne() {
        System.out.println("3. @Test - test one");
    }

    @Test
    void testTwo() {
        System.out.println("3. @Test - test two");
    }

    @AfterEach
    void afterEach() {
        System.out.println("4. @AfterEach - runs after each test");
    }

    @AfterAll
    static void afterAll() {
        System.out.println("5. @AfterAll - runs once after all tests");
    }
}

// Output:
// 1. @BeforeAll - runs once before all tests
// 2. @BeforeEach - runs before each test
// 3. @Test - test one
// 4. @AfterEach - runs after each test
// 2. @BeforeEach - runs before each test
// 3. @Test - test two
// 4. @AfterEach - runs after each test
// 5. @AfterAll - runs once after all tests
```

**Practical Example: Calculator Test with Lifecycle:**

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.*;

class CalculatorTest {

    private Calculator calculator;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        calculator = new Calculator();
        System.out.println("Creating fresh calculator for test");
    }

    @Test
    @DisplayName("Addition of two positive numbers")
    void testAddition() {
        int result = calculator.add(5, 3);
        Assertions.assertEquals(8, result);
    }

    @Test
    @DisplayName("Subtraction of two numbers")
    void testSubtraction() {
        int result = calculator.subtract(5, 3);
        Assertions.assertEquals(2, result);
    }

    @Test
    @DisplayName("Division by zero should throw exception")
    void testDivisionByZero() {
        Assertions.assertThrows(ArithmeticException.class, () -> {
            calculator.divide(10, 0);
        });
    }

    @AfterEach
    void tearDown() {
        System.out.println("Cleaning up after test");
        calculator = null;
    }
}
```

**@DisplayName - Making Tests Readable:**

```java
@Test
@DisplayName("❌ Should reject negative amount withdrawal")
void testNegativeAmountWithdrawal() {
    Account account = new Account(100);

    Assertions.assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -> {
        account.withdraw(-50);
    });
}
```

**@Disabled - Temporarily Skipping Tests:**

```java
@Test
@Disabled("Feature not implemented yet - JIRA-1234")
void testNewFeature() {
    // This test won't run
}

@Disabled("Fixing flaky test - see JIRA-5678")
class FlakyTestSuite {
    // All tests in this class are disabled
}
```

**@Tag - Categorizing Tests:**

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Tag;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

@Tag("unit")
class UnitTests {

    @Test
    @Tag("fast")
    void fastUnitTest() { }

    @Test
    @Tag("slow")
    void slowUnitTest() { }

    @Test
    @Tag("database")
    void databaseUnitTest() { }
}

// Run with: mvn test -Dgroups=fast
```

**@Nested - Hierarchical Test Organization:**

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Nested;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

class UserServiceTest {

    private UserService userService = new UserService();

    @Nested
    @DisplayName("User Creation Tests")
    class CreateUserTests {

        @Test
        @DisplayName("Should create user with valid email")
        void shouldCreateUserWithValidEmail() {
            // Test logic
        }

        @Test
        @DisplayName("Should throw exception with invalid email")
        void shouldThrowWithInvalidEmail() {
            // Test logic
        }
    }

    @Nested
    @DisplayName("User Deletion Tests")
    class DeleteUserTests {

        @Test
        @DisplayName("Should delete existing user")
        void shouldDeleteExistingUser() {
            // Test logic
        }

        @Test
        @DisplayName("Should return false for non-existent user")
        void shouldReturnFalseForNonExistentUser() {
            // Test logic
        }
    }
}
```

**@RepeatedTest - Running Tests Multiple Times:**

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.RepeatedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.RepetitionInfo;

class RepeatedTests {

    @RepeatedTest(5)
    void testRandomValue(RepetitionInfo repetitionInfo) {
        System.out.println("Repetition #" + repetitionInfo.getCurrentRepetition());
        // Test that might need multiple runs
    }

    @RepeatedTest(value = 3, name = "Attempt {currentRepetition} of {totalRepetitions}")
    void testWithCustomName() {
        // Test with better reporting
    }
}
```

**@TestInstance - Controlling Test Instance Lifecycle:**

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.TestInstance;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

@TestInstance(TestInstance.Lifecycle.PER_CLASS)
class SharedStateTest {

    private int counter = 0;

    @Test
    void testOne() {
        counter++;
        System.out.println("Counter: " + counter); // 1
    }

    @Test
    void testTwo() {
        counter++;
        System.out.println("Counter: " + counter); // 2
    }
}

// Default is PER_METHOD (new instance for each test)
```

**@Timeout - Preventing Hanging Tests:**

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Timeout;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

class TimeoutTest {

    @Test
    @Timeout(1) // seconds
    void testWithTimeout() throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.sleep(2000); // This will cause test failure
    }

    @Test
    @Timeout(value = 500, unit = TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)
    void testWithMilliseconds() {
        // Fast test
    }
}
```

**The Heart of Unit Testing:**

Assertions are methods that verify expected outcomes. JUnit Jupiter provides a rich set of assertion methods in the `Assertions` class.

**Core Assertion Methods:**

| Method | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| **assertEquals(expected, actual)** | Checks that two values are equal |
| **assertNotEquals(expected, actual)** | Checks that two values are not equal |
| **assertTrue(condition)** | Checks that a condition is true |
| **assertFalse(condition)** | Checks that a condition is false |
| **assertNull(object)** | Checks that an object is null |
| **assertNotNull(object)** | Checks that an object is not null |
| **assertSame(expected, actual)** | Checks that two objects refer to the same instance |
| **assertNotSame(expected, actual)** | Checks that two objects don't refer to the same instance |
| **assertThrows(exceptionType, executable)** | Checks that an exception is thrown |
| **assertDoesNotThrow(executable)** | Checks that no exception is thrown |
| **assertAll(executables...)** | Groups multiple assertions |
| **assertIterableEquals(expected, actual)** | Checks that two iterables are deeply equal |
| **assertArrayEquals(expected, actual)** | Checks that two arrays are equal |
| **assertLinesMatch(expectedLines, actualLines)** | Checks lines match expected patterns |

**Basic Assertion Examples:**

```java
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

class AssertionExamplesTest {

    @Test
    void testBasicAssertions() {
        // Equality
        assertEquals(4, 2 + 2, "2 + 2 should equal 4");
        assertNotEquals(5, 2 + 2);

        // Booleans
        assertTrue(10 > 5);
        assertFalse(10 < 5);

        // Null checks
        Object obj = null;
        assertNull(obj);
        obj = new Object();
        assertNotNull(obj);

        // Identity
        String s1 = "hello";
        String s2 = "hello";
        assertSame(s1, s2); // String interning

        String s3 = new String("hello");
        assertNotSame(s1, s3); // Different instances
    }
}
```

**Exception Assertions:**

```java
class ExceptionAssertionsTest {

    @Test
    void testExceptionThrown() {
        Exception exception = assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -> {
            new Account().withdraw(-100);
        });

        assertEquals("Amount must be positive", exception.getMessage());
    }

    @Test
    void testNoExceptionThrown() {
        assertDoesNotThrow(() -> {
            new Account().withdraw(100);
        });
    }

    @Test
    void testSpecificExceptionType() {
        assertThrows(ArithmeticException.class, () -> {
            int result = 10 / 0;
        });
    }
}
```

**Grouped Assertions with assertAll:**

```java
class GroupedAssertionsTest {

    @Test
    void testUserProperties() {
        User user = new User("john@example.com", "John", 30);

        // All assertions run, even if some fail
        assertAll("User properties",
            () -> assertEquals("john@example.com", user.getEmail()),
            () -> assertEquals("John", user.getName()),
            () -> assertEquals(30, user.getAge()),
            () -> assertTrue(user.isActive())
        );
    }

    @Test
    void testOrderProperties() {
        Order order = createTestOrder();

        assertAll("Order validation",
            () -> assertNotNull(order.getId()),
            () -> assertFalse(order.getItems().isEmpty()),
            () -> assertTrue(order.getTotal() > 0),
            () -> assertEquals(OrderStatus.PENDING, order.getStatus())
        );
    }
}
```

**Array and Collection Assertions:**

```java
class CollectionAssertionsTest {

    @Test
    void testArrayAssertions() {
        int[] expected = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        int[] actual = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

        assertArrayEquals(expected, actual, "Arrays should be equal");
    }

    @Test
    void testIterableAssertions() {
        List<String> expected = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");
        List<String> actual = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");

        assertIterableEquals(expected, actual, "Lists should be equal");

        // Order matters
        List<String> differentOrder = Arrays.asList("c", "b", "a");
        assertIterableEquals(expected, differentOrder); // This would fail
    }

    @Test
    void testLineAssertions() {
        List<String> expected = Arrays.asList("Hello", "World");
        List<String> actual = Arrays.asList("Hello", "World");

        assertLinesMatch(expected, actual);
    }
}
```

**Floating Point Assertions:**

```java
class FloatingPointAssertionsTest {

    @Test
    void testFloatingPoint() {
        double result = 10.0 / 3.0;

        // With delta (tolerance)
        assertEquals(3.333, result, 0.001);

        // Without delta (dangerous!)
        // assertEquals(3.333333333, result); // Might fail due to precision
    }
}
```

**Timeout Assertions:**

```java
import java.time.Duration;

class TimeoutAssertionsTest {

    @Test
    void testTimeout() {
        // Assert that execution completes within specified time
        assertTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(1), () -> {
            // Code that should complete within 1 second
            Thread.sleep(500);
        });
    }

    @Test
    void testTimeoutPreemptively() {
        // Preemptively fails if timeout exceeded
        assertTimeoutPreemptively(Duration.ofMillis(100), () -> {
            Thread.sleep(200); // This will cause immediate failure
        });
    }
}
```

**Custom Assertion Messages:**

```java
class MessageAssertionsTest {

    @Test
    void testWithMessages() {
        // Simple message
        assertEquals(5, 2 + 2, "2 + 2 should equal 5"); // Fails with message

        // Lazy message (only evaluated if assertion fails)
        assertEquals(5, 2 + 2, () -> 
            String.format("Expected %d but got %d", 5, 2 + 2)
        );

        // Detailed message with values
        int expected = 5;
        int actual = 2 + 2;
        assertEquals(expected, actual, 
            () -> String.format("Expected %d but was %d", expected, actual));
    }
}
```

**Best Practices for Assertions:**

| Practice | Example | Why |
|----------|---------|-----|
| **Use meaningful messages** | `assertEquals(expected, actual, "User count mismatch")` | Helps debug failures |
| **One assertion per test** | Avoid testing multiple unrelated things | Clear failure reporting |
| **Use assertAll for multiple checks** | Group related assertions | Get all failures at once |
| **Test both positive and negative** | `assertTrue` and `assertFalse` | Complete coverage |
| **Assert exceptions** | `assertThrows` for error conditions | Verify error handling |
| **Use deltas for doubles** | `assertEquals(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3, 0.0001)` | Avoid precision issues |
| **Lazy messages** | Use lambda for expensive messages | Performance optimization |
| **Be specific** | `assertEquals` vs `assertTrue` | Clearer intent |

**Running Multiple Tests Together:**

Test suites allow you to group related test classes and run them together. This is useful for organizing tests by feature, module, or test type.

**JUnit 5 Test Suites:**

In JUnit 5, test suites are created using the `@SelectPackages` or `@SelectClasses` annotations.

**1. Suite by Packages:**

```java
import org.junit.platform.suite.api.SelectPackages;
import org.junit.platform.suite.api.Suite;

@Suite
@SelectPackages({
    "com.example.user",
    "com.example.order",
    "com.example.payment"
})
public class AllTestsSuite {
    // Empty class - just for configuration
}
```

**2. Suite by Classes:**

```java
import org.junit.platform.suite.api.SelectClasses;
import org.junit.platform.suite.api.Suite;

@Suite
@SelectClasses({
    UserServiceTest.class,
    OrderServiceTest.class,
    PaymentServiceTest.class,
    CalculatorTest.class
})
public class UnitTestSuite {
    // Empty class
}
```

**3. Suite with Filters:**

```java
import org.junit.platform.suite.api.*;

@Suite
@SelectPackages("com.example")
@IncludeTags({"smoke", "critical"})
@ExcludeTags({"slow", "flaky"})
@IncludeClassNamePatterns({".*Test"})
@ExcludeClassNamePatterns({".*IntegrationTest"})
public class SmokeTestSuite {
    // Only smoke and critical tests run
}
```

**4. Multiple Suites with Inheritance:**

```java
// Base suite configuration
@Suite
@SelectPackages("com.example")
@IncludeTags("unit")
public interface UnitTestBase { }

// Extended suite
@Suite
@SelectClasses(IntegrationTest.class)
public class FullTestSuite extends UnitTestBase {
    // Runs unit tests + integration test
}
```

**5. Suite with Display Names:**

```java
@Suite
@DisplayName("Complete Test Suite")
@SelectPackages({
    "com.example.unit",
    "com.example.integration"
})
@ExcludeTags("broken")
public class CompleteTestSuite {
    // Will be shown with custom name in reports
}
```

**JUnit 4 Style Suites (Vintage):**

```java
import org.junit.runner.RunWith;
import org.junit.runners.Suite;

@RunWith(Suite.class)
@Suite.SuiteClasses({
    UserServiceTest.class,
    OrderServiceTest.class,
    PaymentServiceTest.class
})
public class JUnit4TestSuite {
    // JUnit 4 style suite
}
```

**Practical Example: E-Commerce Test Organization**

```java
// Unit tests for each module
package com.example.user;

@Suite
@SelectPackages("com.example.user")
@IncludeTags("unit")
public class UserUnitTests { }

package com.example.order;

@Suite
@SelectPackages("com.example.order")
@IncludeTags("unit")
public class OrderUnitTests { }

// Integration tests
package com.example.integration;

@Suite
@SelectPackages({
    "com.example.user",
    "com.example.order",
    "com.example.payment"
})
@IncludeTags("integration")
public class IntegrationTests { }

// Smoke tests (critical path)
package com.example.smoke;

@Suite
@SelectPackages({
    "com.example.user",
    "com.example.order",
    "com.example.payment"
})
@IncludeTags({"smoke", "critical"})
public class SmokeTests { }

// Full regression suite
package com.example.regression;

@Suite
@SelectPackages({
    "com.example.user",
    "com.example.order",
    "com.example.payment",
    "com.example.inventory"
})
@ExcludeTags({"broken", "flaky"})
public class RegressionTests { }
```

**Running Suites with Maven:**

```xml
<plugin>
    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
    <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
    <configuration>
        <includes>
            <include>**/*TestSuite.java</include>
        </includes>
    </configuration>
</plugin>
```

**Running Suites with Gradle:**

```groovy
test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
    filter {
        includeTestsMatching '*TestSuite'
    }
}
```

**Benefits of Test Suites:**

| Benefit | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| **Organization** | Group related tests logically |
| **Selective Execution** | Run specific groups (smoke, regression) |
| **Faster Feedback** | Run different suites at different pipeline stages |
| **Clean Reports** | Reports organized by suite |
| **Reusability** | Suite configurations can be reused |
| **CI/CD Integration** | Different suites for different build stages |

**CI/CD Integration Example:**

```yaml
# GitHub Actions workflow
name: Test
on: [push]

jobs:
  smoke-tests:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v2
      - name: Run smoke tests
        run: mvn test -Dtest=SmokeTests

  regression-tests:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v2
      - name: Run regression tests
        run: mvn test -Dtest=RegressionTests
```

**Best Practices for Test Suites:**

1. **Organize by feature/module** - Keep tests with related functionality
2. **Create type-based suites** - unit, integration, smoke, regression
3. **Use tags for fine-grained control** - Combine with suites for flexibility
4. **Name suites descriptively** - Clear purpose in the name
5. **Keep suites maintainable** - Update as project evolves
6. **Document suite purpose** - Comments explaining what the suite covers
7. **Use in CI/CD** - Different suites for different pipeline stages

