**Proven Practices for Test Excellence**

Following these best practices ensures your tests are maintainable, reliable, and provide value throughout the project lifecycle.

**1. Test Independence**

Each test should be able to run alone, in any order, without affecting others.

```java
// BAD - Tests depend on each other
class DependentTest {
    private static User createdUser;

    @Test
    void testCreateUser() {
        createdUser = userService.createUser("test@example.com");
        assertNotNull(createdUser);
    }

    @Test
    void testFindUser() {  // Depends on testCreateUser running first!
        User found = userService.findUserById(createdUser.getId());
        assertNotNull(found);
    }
}

// GOOD - Independent tests
class IndependentTest {

    @Test
    void testCreateUser() {
        User user = userService.createUser("test@example.com");
        assertNotNull(user);
    }

    @Test
    void testFindUser() {
        // Setup own data
        User user = userService.createUser("test@example.com");
        User found = userService.findUserById(user.getId());
        assertNotNull(found);
    }
}
```

**2. One Behavior Per Test**

Each test should verify one specific behavior.

```java
// BAD - Testing multiple things
@Test
void testUser() {
    User user = userService.createUser("test@example.com", "Test");
    assertNotNull(user);
    assertEquals("test@example.com", user.getEmail());
    assertEquals("Test", user.getName());
    assertTrue(userService.findUserById(user.getId()) != null);
    assertTrue(userService.deleteUser(user.getId()));
}

// GOOD - Focused tests
@Test
void createUser_shouldReturnUserWithEmail() {
    User user = userService.createUser("test@example.com", "Test");
    assertEquals("test@example.com", user.getEmail());
}

@Test
void createUser_shouldReturnUserWithName() {
    User user = userService.createUser("test@example.com", "Test");
    assertEquals("Test", user.getName());
}

@Test
void findUser_shouldReturnUser_whenExists() {
    User user = userService.createUser("test@example.com", "Test");
    User found = userService.findUserById(user.getId());
    assertNotNull(found);
}
```

**3. Use Meaningful Names**

Test names should describe the scenario and expected outcome.

```java
// BAD - Unclear names
@Test
void test1() { }

@Test
void testLogin() { }

// GOOD - Descriptive names
@Test
void shouldReturnUser_whenValidCredentialsProvided() { }

@Test
void shouldThrowException_whenPasswordIsNull() { }

@Test
void shouldReturnFalse_whenUserDoesNotExist() { }
```

**4. Keep Tests Fast**

Unit tests should run quickly (milliseconds, not seconds).

```java
// BAD - Slow test
@Test
void testDatabase() throws Exception {
    Thread.sleep(1000);  // Don't sleep!
    // Real database call - use mocking instead
}

// GOOD - Fast test with mocking
@Test
void testService() {
    UserRepository mockRepo = mock(UserRepository.class);
    when(mockRepo.findById(1)).thenReturn(new User("test"));

    UserService service = new UserService(mockRepo);
    User user = service.findUser(1);

    assertEquals("test", user.getName());
}
```

**5. Use Fixtures Wisely**

```java
class FixtureTest {

    private UserService userService;
    private User testUser;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        userService = new UserService();
        testUser = userService.createUser("test@example.com", "Test");
    }

