**Summary: Keys to Great Test Classes**

1. **One test class per production class** - Clear mapping
2. **Use @Nested for logical grouping** - Better organization
3. **Descriptive test names** - Self-documenting
4. **Independent tests** - No shared mutable state
5. **Fast execution** - Mock external dependencies
6. **Comprehensive coverage** - Happy paths and edge cases
7. **Readable assertions** - Clear verification
8. **Proper setup/teardown** - Clean test fixtures
9. **Follow naming conventions** - Consistency across team
10. **Regular refactoring** - Keep tests maintainable

**Assertions: The Heart of Test Automation**

Assertions are statements that verify whether the actual outcome of a test matches the expected outcome. Without assertions, tests can run but provide no validation—they're just executing code without checking results.

**Why Assertions Matter:**

| Benefit | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| **Validation** | Confirm that the system behaves as expected |
| **Documentation** | Assertions document what the test verifies |
| **Failure Detection** | Automatically detect when something goes wrong |
| **Regression Prevention** | Catch unintended changes in behavior |
| **Quality Gates** | Provide pass/fail criteria for CI/CD pipelines |

**The JUnit Assertions Class:**

JUnit 5 provides the `org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions` class with static methods for all common assertion types. Simply import it statically and use the methods directly.

```java
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

class AssertionExample {

    @Test
    void testSomething() {
        int actual = 5;
        int expected = 5;

        // Basic assertion
        assertEquals(expected, actual, "Values should be equal");
    }
}
```

**Assertion Method Categories:**

| Category | Purpose | Example Methods |
|----------|---------|-----------------|
| **Equality** | Compare values for equality | `assertEquals`, `assertNotEquals` |
| **Boolean** | Verify true/false conditions | `assertTrue`, `assertFalse` |
| **Null Checks** | Verify null/non-null | `assertNull`, `assertNotNull` |
| **Object Identity** | Check object references | `assertSame`, `assertNotSame` |
| **Arrays/Collections** | Compare arrays and collections | `assertArrayEquals`, `assertIterableEquals` |
| **Exceptions** | Verify exception throwing | `assertThrows`, `assertDoesNotThrow` |
| **Timeouts** | Verify execution time | `assertTimeout`, `assertTimeoutPreemptively` |
| **Grouped** | Multiple assertions together | `assertAll` |
| **Fail** | Force test failure | `fail` |

**Assertion Lifecycle in a Test:**

```java
@Test
void testUserCreation() {
    // Arrange
    UserService userService = new UserService();
    User newUser = new User("john@example.com", "John Doe");

    // Act
    User createdUser = userService.createUser(newUser);

    // Assert - Multiple assertions verify different aspects
    assertNotNull(createdUser, "User should not be null");
    assertNotNull(createdUser.getId(), "User ID should be generated");
    assertEquals("john@example.com", createdUser.getEmail(), "Email should match");
    assertEquals("John Doe", createdUser.getName(), "Name should match");
    assertTrue(createdUser.isActive(), "User should be active by default");
}
```

**What Happens When Assertions Fail:**

When an assertion fails, JUnit throws an `AssertionFailedError`. This:
1. Immediately stops the current test method
2. Marks the test as FAILED in the report
3. Records the assertion error message and stack trace
4. Continues with the next test method

```java
@Test
void testThatWillFail() {
    int actual = 5;
    int expected = 10;

    // This will throw AssertionFailedError with the message
    assertEquals(expected, actual, 
        "Expected 10 but got 5");

    // This line will never execute
    System.out.println("This won't print");
}
```

**Assertion Messages:**

Always include descriptive messages to help debug failures.

```java
// Bad - no context when it fails
assertTrue(user.isActive());

// Good - clear what failed
assertTrue(user.isActive(), 
    "User should be active after registration");

// Even better - include actual values
assertTrue(user.isActive(), 
    () -> String.format("User %s should be active but was %s", 
        user.getEmail(), user.getStatus()));

// Lazy evaluation - message only constructed if assertion fails
assertTrue(user.isActive(), 
    () -> expensiveMessageConstruction());
```

