**Practical Example: E-Commerce Order Validation**

```java
class OrderValidationMethodSourceTest {

    private OrderValidator validator = new OrderValidator();

    @ParameterizedTest(name = "Order {0} validation should be {1}")
    @MethodSource("orderTestData")
    void testOrderValidation(Order order, boolean expectedValid, String expectedError) {
        ValidationResult result = validator.validate(order);

        assertEquals(expectedValid, result.isValid());
        if (!expectedValid) {
            assertTrue(result.getErrors().contains(expectedError));
        }
    }

    static Stream<Arguments> orderTestData() {
        User validUser = new User("user@test.com", "John");
        User invalidUser = new User(null, "Jane");

        Product laptop = new Product("Laptop", 999.99);
        Product mouse = new Product("Mouse", 29.99);
        Product outOfStockProduct = new Product("Out of Stock", 49.99, 0);

        return Stream.of(
            // Valid orders
            Arguments.of(
                new Order(validUser, Arrays.asList(new OrderItem(laptop, 1))),
                true, null
            ),
            Arguments.of(
                new Order(validUser, Arrays.asList(
                    new OrderItem(laptop, 1),
                    new OrderItem(mouse, 2)
                )),
                true, null
            ),

            // Invalid orders
            Arguments.of(
                new Order(null, Arrays.asList(new OrderItem(laptop, 1))),
                false, "Missing user"
            ),
            Arguments.of(
                new Order(validUser, Arrays.asList()),
                false, "Empty order"
            ),
            Arguments.of(
                new Order(validUser, Arrays.asList(
                    new OrderItem(laptop, 0)
                )),
                false, "Invalid quantity"
            ),
            Arguments.of(
                new Order(validUser, Arrays.asList(
                    new OrderItem(outOfStockProduct, 1)
                )),
                false, "Out of stock"
            ),
            Arguments.of(
                new Order(validUser, Arrays.asList(
                    new OrderItem(laptop, -1)
                )),
                false, "Negative quantity"
            )
        );
    }
}
```

**Best Practices for MethodSource:**

| Practice | Example | Benefit |
|----------|---------|---------|
| **Keep providers separate** | External class for providers | Reusability |
| **Use descriptive method names** | `validUserProvider()` | Self-documenting |
| **Document complex data** | Comments in provider | Maintainability |
| **Cache expensive data** | Static initialization | Performance |
| **Use Arguments.of** | Clear syntax | Readability |
| **Limit provider size** | Split large providers | Manageability |
| **Test providers separately** | Unit tests for providers | Reliability |
| **Handle exceptions gracefully** | Try-catch in providers | Robustness |

**Taking Data-Driven Testing to the Next Level**

**1. Custom Argument Providers with @ArgumentsSource**

For maximum reusability, create custom argument provider classes.

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.Arguments;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.ArgumentsProvider;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.support.AnnotationConsumer;

class CustomArgumentsProvider implements ArgumentsProvider {

    @Override
    public Stream<? extends Arguments> provideArguments(ExtensionContext context) {
        return Stream.of(
            Arguments.of(1, 2, 3),
            Arguments.of(4, 5, 9),
            Arguments.of(10, -5, 5)
        );
    }
}

class CustomProviderTest {

    @ParameterizedTest
    @ArgumentsSource(CustomArgumentsProvider.class)
    void testWithCustomProvider(int a, int b, int expected) {
        assertEquals(expected, a + b);
    }
}
```

**Parameterized Custom Provider:**

```java
import java.lang.annotation.ElementType;
import java.lang.annotation.Retention;
import java.lang.annotation.RetentionPolicy;
import java.lang.annotation.Target;

@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@ArgumentsSource(FileArgumentsProvider.class)
@interface FileSource {
    String value();
}

class FileArgumentsProvider implements ArgumentsProvider, AnnotationConsumer<FileSource> {

    private String fileName;

    @Override
    public void accept(FileSource fileSource) {
        this.fileName = fileSource.value();
    }

    @Override
    public Stream<? extends Arguments> provideArguments(ExtensionContext context) 
            throws Exception {
        List<Arguments> args = new ArrayList<>();
        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(
                new InputStreamReader(
                    getClass().getResourceAsStream(fileName)))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                String[] parts = line.split(",");
                args.add(Arguments.of((Object[]) parts));
            }
        }
        return args.stream();
    }
}

class CustomAnnotationTest {

    @ParameterizedTest
    @FileSource("/test-data.txt")
    void testWithCustomAnnotation(String first, String second) {
        assertNotNull(first);
        assertNotNull(second);
    }
}
