**When to Use @BeforeEach:**

| Scenario | Use @BeforeEach | Alternative |
|----------|-----------------|-------------|
| **Initialize fresh objects** | ✅ Yes - each test needs new instances | @BeforeAll if objects are immutable |
| **Reset mutable state** | ✅ Yes - prevent test interference | @BeforeAll not suitable |
| **Create test data** | ✅ Yes - each test needs its own data | @BeforeAll for read-only data |
| **Open database connections** | ❌ No - too expensive | @BeforeAll instead |
| **Load configuration** | ❌ No - once per class is enough | @BeforeAll |

**When to Use @AfterEach:**

| Scenario | Use @AfterEach | Alternative |
|----------|----------------|-------------|
| **Delete test files** | ✅ Yes - clean up per test | @AfterAll for shared files |
| **Close browser windows** | ✅ Yes - fresh browser per test | @AfterAll for single browser |
| **Clear test data** | ✅ Yes - prevent data pollution | @AfterAll for bulk cleanup |
| **Close database connections** | ❌ No - too expensive | @AfterAll instead |
| **Generate test reports** | ❌ No - better at class level | @AfterAll |

**One-Time Setup for Expensive Resources**

`@BeforeAll` and `@AfterAll` methods run once per test class, before any tests and after all tests. They are perfect for expensive operations that can be shared across multiple tests.

**Basic Example:**

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.*;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

class DatabaseTest {

    private static DatabaseConnection connection;
    private static TestDataLoader dataLoader;

    @BeforeAll
    static void initDatabase() {
        // Runs once before any tests
        System.out.println("Connecting to database...");
        connection = new DatabaseConnection();
        connection.connect("jdbc:mysql://localhost/testdb");

        dataLoader = new TestDataLoader();
        dataLoader.loadFixtures();
    }

    @Test
    void testQuery1() {
        ResultSet rs = connection.query("SELECT * FROM users");
        assertNotNull(rs);
        assertTrue(rs.getRowCount() > 0);
    }

    @Test
    void testQuery2() {
        ResultSet rs = connection.query("SELECT * FROM products");
        assertNotNull(rs);
        assertTrue(rs.getRowCount() > 0);
    }

    @AfterAll
    static void closeDatabase() {
        // Runs once after all tests
        System.out.println("Closing database connection...");
        if (connection != null) {
            connection.close();
        }
        if (dataLoader != null) {
            dataLoader.cleanup();
        }
    }
}
```

