**Error Handling in @BeforeAll and @AfterAll:**

```java
class ErrorHandlingTest {

    private static Server server;

    @BeforeAll
    static void startServer() {
        try {
            server = new Server();
            server.start();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Log error and fail the whole test suite
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to start server", e);
        }
    }

    @Test
    void test1() {
        // If @BeforeAll fails, this test won't run
        assertTrue(server.isRunning());
    }

    @AfterAll
    static void stopServer() {
        if (server != null) {
            try {
                server.stop();
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // Log but don't throw - test suite already finished
                System.err.println("Error stopping server: " + e.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**When to Use @BeforeAll vs @BeforeEach:**

| Criteria | @BeforeAll | @BeforeEach |
|----------|------------|-------------|
| **Execution frequency** | Once per class | Once per test |
| **Resource cost** | Expensive operations | Cheap operations |
| **State sharing** | Shared across tests | Isolated per test |
| **Thread safety** | Must be thread-safe | Instance per thread |
| **Test data** | Read-only data | Mutable data |
| **External services** | Connection pooling | Per-test connections |

**Decision Tree:**

```
Do you need to initialize something for your tests?
                │
                ▼
    Is it expensive (DB, server, file loading)?
                │
        ┌───────┴───────┐
        ▼               ▼
       YES              NO
        │               │
        ▼               ▼
    @BeforeAll      @BeforeEach
        │               │
        ▼               ▼
  Can it be shared  Does each test need
  across tests?     fresh state?
        │               │
    ┌───┴───┐       ┌───┴───┐
    ▼       ▼       ▼       ▼
   YES      NO     YES      NO
    │       │      │        │
    ▼       ▼      ▼        ▼
 @BeforeAll @BeforeEach @BeforeEach @BeforeAll
```

